<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Campaign</p><h1 class="enc-title">Allied invasion of Sicily</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1943">1943</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Allied invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> campaign in 1943 in which British, American and Canadian forces landed on and captured Sicily. The invasion contributed to the overthrow of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Mussolini</a> in July 1943. It was a key step in the collapse of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fascist-italy">Fascist Italy</a> and the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-empire">Italian Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/allied-invasion-of-sicily.jpg" alt="Allied invasion of Sicily" width="320" height="248" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: United States Military Academy · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_operation_husky_landing.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The campaign in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> ended in May 1943, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> had for the first time driven the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis powers</a> from a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> of war. Led by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and the United Kingdom, they chose to attack Axis forces in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> rather than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-europe">Western Europe</a>, citing wavering Italian morale, their control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> sea lanes and the vulnerability of German supply lines along the Italian peninsula. Operation Mincemeat was the most successful of the deception operations.</p><p>Operation Husky planned two armies to land on the south-eastern and central southern coasts, supported by naval gunfire and by tactical bombing. General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dwight-d-eisenhower">Dwight D. Eisenhower</a> was overall commander, with General Sir Harold Alexander commanding the 15th Army Group. The Eastern Task Force was General Sir Bernard Montgomery's British Eighth Army, including the 1st Canadian Infantry Division, and the Western Task Force was Lieutenant General George S. Patton's American Seventh Army. The island was defended by the Italian 6th Army and the German XIV Panzer Corps.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The operation began on the night of 9/10 July 1943 with a large amphibious and airborne assault. Strong winds of up to 70 km/h blew the troop-carrying aircraft off course. The American paratroopers, making their first combat drop, were scattered widely between Gela and Syracuse, and only 12 of 147 British gliders landed on target, while 69 crashed into the sea and over 200 men drowned. Even so, scattered small units attacked vital points on their own initiative and caused confusion.</p><p>A platoon of the 2nd Battalion, South Staffordshire Regiment, under Lieutenant Louis Withers took the Ponte Grande bridge south of Syracuse and held it against counterattacks. By the time it surrendered, reduced to 18 men, the leading British 5th Division troops were 45 minutes away. The land campaign lasted six weeks and ended on 17 August.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> drove <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis</a> air, land and naval forces from the island, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> was open to Allied merchant ships for the first time since 1941. The invasion led to the ousting of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Benito Mussolini</a> and the fall of his regime, replaced by the first Badoglio government. German troops had to take over from Italian forces in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and, to a lesser extent, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a>, diverting one-fifth of the German army from the Eastern Front. The invasion opened the way to the invasion of mainland Italy.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis</a> forces evacuated the island: the Italians some 62,182 men by one account, the Germans about 52,000. The U.S. Seventh Army lost 8,781 men and the British Eighth Army 11,843, and Canadian forces had 2,310 casualties. German losses are given as 27,940 in one study.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Allied invasion of Sicily” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The final blow came in July 1943 with the <b>Allied invasion of Sicily</b>; following a vote of the Fascist Grand Council, King Victor Emmanuel III dismissed and arrested Mussolini.</blockquote><a href="/empire/italian-empire">Italian Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
